<Profile title="Approach — Dave Bettner" page="fit">
  <section class="page-intro wrap">
    <h1>Start with the workflow.<br/>Stay through delivery.</h1>
    <p class="lead">Where does the data come from? What needs to happen next? Who can approve a change? I start with the customer’s actual work, then work through the software, integration, and adoption details.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="paper">
    <div class="section wrap prose-grid">
      <p class="aside">My strongest work sits between the customer, the software, and the team responsible for making it work.</p>
      <div class="prose">
        <section>
          <h2>Understand the problem before proposing a solution.</h2>
          <p>I run requirements sessions with the people using the system. We work through workflows, data sources, access, exceptions, and what success would look like. The aim is a concrete problem to solve, not a list of features to configure.</p>
        </section>
        <section id="scope">
          <h2>Set scope around client ownership.</h2>
          <p>In a recent implementation, I reset expectations around who would do the work. Our role was to enable the client team and help them get comfortable doing it themselves, rather than do all of the work for them.</p>
        </section>
        <section>
          <h2>Turn recurring delivery work into reusable tools.</h2>
          <p>At LSL, I develop Python tools that generate reporting links, formulas, and validation checks. They are reused across financial-reporting and budgeting engagements. AI-assisted checks flag source discrepancies for review, with human review before client changes.</p>
        </section>
        <section>
          <h2>Carry the work through acceptance.</h2>
          <p>I stay involved through user acceptance testing, training, and go-live. Consultant coaching and repeatable delivery guidance help the team support the implementation. Recurring customer issues also become specific feedback for Product and Engineering.</p>
          <a href="/experience/connected-reporting/">See a connected-reporting implementation</a>
        </section>
        <section>
          <h2>Choose where automation belongs.</h2>
          <p>In Leasekite, I keep routine status updates tied to recorded quote and delivery state. The application prepares drafts for review; questions and negotiation still need human judgment. That distinction shapes the workflow I’m building.</p>
          <a href="/work/">See the technical projects</a>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Profile>
